1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian game is one of the oldest openings in chess. It was developed in the 16th and 17th centuries by famous Italian masters Damiano, Polerio, and Greco. It has always been frequently seen in beginners' and amateurs' games but its popularity among strong players gradually ceased in favor of the Ruy Lopez, which was considered a better try for an advantage until very recently. However, things have changed dramatically in recent years. Due to the futility of their attempts against the Berlin Defence, top players have started seeking ways to pose Black problems in the long-forgotten Italian. As a result of the trend set by Carlsen, Anand, Caruana, Karjakin, Vachier- Lagrave, Giri and other strong players it has suddenly become more popular than the Ruy Lopez and its theory has grown enormously. Unlike just a few years ago, Black is nowadays required to know the nuances of different lines and move orders to obtain a good position out of the opening. } Bc5 4. O-O ( { With } 4. c3 { we saw White either playing for a quick d4 or he delayed castling but the natural } 4. O-O { is the more popular way of playing the Italian. } ) 
Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. c3 { -KEY- } d5 { The most active approach, reminiscent to the variation I recommend against 4. d3 anti-Berlin main line. } 7. exd5 Nxd5 { We have reached one of the most important structures in this repertoire. White mainly plays against the e5-pawn and sometimes grabs space on the queenside. Black, on the other hand seeks counterplay against the d3-pawn and on the kingside, where he has the pawn majority. Typical continuations include ...Nb6, ...Bg4, ...Bd6 or ...f6 (after ...Nb6). } 8. a4 ( { The most challenging move, favored by elite players. The idea is quite cunning. After } 8. a4 Bg4 { , White wants to stop the typical ...Nb6 resource by } 9. a5 $1 ) 
a5 ( { Possible is } 8... Nb6 9. Bb5 Ne7 $1 { introduced by Wesley So. White light-squared bishop is such an important piece, that it is worth sacrificing a pawn to eliminate it. It's not a move one could come up with over the board. We just recommend a simpler approach. } ) 
9. Nbd2 Nb6 10. Bb5 Bd6 { This should transpose to other lines. } 11. Ne4 ( 11. Re1 { directly transposes to 9.Re1 } ) 
Bg4 ( { Also possible is forcing } 11... Na7 12. d4 Nxb5 13. axb5 exd4 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. Qxd4 Bd7 { with a drawish endgame. } ) 
12. h3 Bh5 ( { Whatever White plays, } 12... Bh5 12. Re1 f5 { (see here) } ( { or } 12... Bh5 12. Ng3 Bg6 { (see here) (we will transpose to lines covered in this repertoire.) } ) 
 ) 

